\end{tabular}</p>
      <p><term>Instructions:</term> The exercises here should be completed in groups
of 2 or 3 students with one write-up submitted from each group.
You may write directly on this handout.  When asked for an
explanation, please use complete sentences.  Be sure to include
everyone's name on the write-up.</p>
      <p>%  We have seen that the definite integral is defined through a
% summing process.  We will usually evaluate definite
% integrals using the Fundamental Theorem: this is a wondeful fact which
% says we can replace the tedious summing process by
% antidifferentiation.  However, it turns out that there are many
% interesting functions whose antiderivatives cannot be expressed in
% terms of functions we already know.  For this reason, the Fundamental
% Theorem is not much help in these cases.</p>
      <p>In this lab, we'll explore some techniques for approximating definite
integrals.  In many instances, this is as useful, and even easier,
than applying the Fundamental Theorem.</p>
      <p>\begin{itemize}
\item[1.]   Evaluate the definite integral
  <md>
  \int_0^1 \frac 4{1+x^2}~dx
  </md>
  <em>exactly</em>;  that is, do not give a decimal approximation to the
  integral.  (You will need to remember one fact from trigonometry;
  ask if you don't remember.)</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>\item[2.]  In this lab, we will explore three ways of approximating
  definite integrals.  This is typically done when the integrand
  cannot practically be antidifferentiated.  However, we will study
  the integral above;  since we know the exact answer, we
  can determine how accurate our approximations are.</p>
      <p>First, we'll use <em>right</em> endpoints to approximate the definite
  integral.  This means that the height of the rectangles we use to
  approximate the definite integral are computed using the right
  endpoints of the smaller intervals as shown below
  (<m>\Delta x</m> here is 0.25 so that the rectangles appear clearly):</p>
      <p><image source="figures/right3.eps"/></p>
      <p>Set up a Google sheet, as you did in the last lab,
  to use right endpoints to approximate the integral using
  <m>n = 10</m> rectangles.  The first part of your worksheet might look
  like this:</p>
      <p>\begin{tabular}{|r|r|r|r|}
      \hline
      <m>\Delta x</m> &amp; <m>x_i</m> &amp; <m>f(x_i)</m> &amp; <m>\sum f(x_i) \Delta x</m> 
      \hline
      0.1 &amp;    0.0&amp;     4.0000 &amp;     
      0.1 &amp;    0.1&amp;     3.9603 &amp;      0.396039 
      0.1 &amp;    0.2&amp;     3.8461 &amp;      0.780654 
      0.1 &amp;    0.3&amp;     3.6697 &amp;      1.147627 
      0.1 &amp;    0.4&amp;     3.4482 &amp;      1.492455 
      0.1 &amp;    0.5&amp;     3.2000 &amp;      1.812455 
      0.1 &amp;    0.6&amp;     2.9411 &amp;      2.106572 
      0.1 &amp;    0.7&amp;     2.6845 &amp;      2.375029 
      0.1 &amp;    0.8&amp;     2.4390 &amp;      2.618931 
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
      <p>Using <em>right</em> endpoints, find the approximations to the definite
  integral when <m>n=10</m> (<m>\Delta x =0.1</m>), <m>n=20</m> (<m>\Delta x=0.05</m>) and
  <m>n=40</m> (<m>\Delta x=0.025</m>).  For each of these cases, record the
  approximation and the error.  (Note:  if you
  set up your worksheet well, you should only have to change one entry
  to study the different cases.)</p>
      <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      <m>\Delta x</m> &amp; Approximation &amp; Error 
      \hline
      0.1 &amp;  &amp;   
      0.05 &amp; &amp;  
      0.025 &amp; &amp; 
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
      <p>\item[3.]  We will now study a new method, called the Midpoint Rule,
  in which we use the midpoints of the small intervals rather than the
  right endpoints.  That is, the height of the rectangles we use to
  approximate the definite integral are computed by looking at the
  middle of an interval as shown below:</p>
      <p><image source="figures/mid3.eps"/></p>
      <p>If we let <m>n=10</m>, here is how your spreadsheet
  should begin.</p>
      <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
  \hline
  <m>\Delta x</m>&amp;<m>x_i</m>&amp;<m>f(x_i)</m>&amp; <m>\sum f(x_i)\Delta x</m>
  \hline
  0.1000&amp;0.0500&amp;3.9900&amp;0.399002
  0.1000&amp;0.1500&amp;3.9120&amp;0.790201
  0.1000&amp;0.2500&amp;3.7647&amp;1.166671
  0.1000&amp;0.3500&amp;3.5635&amp;1.523019
  0.1000&amp;0.4500&amp;3.3264&amp;1.855659
  0.1000&amp;0.5500&amp;3.0710&amp;2.162761
  0.1000&amp;0.6500&amp;2.8120&amp;2.443956
  0.1000&amp;0.7500&amp;2.5600&amp;2.699956
  \hline
\end{tabular}</p>
      <p>Use the Midpoint Rule with <m>n=10</m>, <m>20</m> and <m>40</m> to approximate the
integral.  For each value of <m>n</m>, record your approximation and the
error.</p>
      <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      <m>\Delta x</m> &amp; Approximation &amp; Error 
      \hline
      0.1 &amp;  &amp;   
      0.05 &amp; &amp;  
      0.025 &amp; &amp; 
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
      <p>\item[4.]  Finally, we will consider the <em>Trapezoidal Rule.</em>  
  Now instead of just using rectangles, we will use trapezoids to
  approximate the integral as shown below:</p>
      <p><image source="figures/trap3.eps"/></p>
      <p>% Before we go any further, find the area of the trapezoid
  % below.  Perhaps the best way to do this is realize that it has the
  % same area as the rectangle shown.  What is the height of this
  % rectangle and hence what is its area?</p>
      <p>You may have seen this before, but it's helpful to remember that the
  area of the trapezoid shown below is 
  <md>
  \frac h2(y_0+y_1).
  </md></p>
      <p><image source="figures/trap3a.eps"/> 
%    
%    \epsfbox[100 400 200 550]{trap3b.eps}</p>
      <p>Now build an Google sheet to compute an approximation to the
  definite integral using <m>n = 10</m> trapezoids or <m>\Delta x = 0.1</m>.
  The fourth column should contain the sum of the areas of the
  trapezoids;  for instance, the second row should contain the area of
  the first trapezoid, the third row should contain the area of the
  first two trapezoids, etc.  Your worksheet should begin something
  like this:</p>
      <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      <m>\Delta x</m> &amp; <m>x_i</m> &amp; <m>f(x_i)</m> &amp; Area of trapezoids 
      \hline
      0.1 &amp;    0.0&amp;     4.000000 &amp;     
      0.1 &amp;    0.1&amp;     3.960396 &amp;     0.398019 
      0.1 &amp;    0.2&amp;     3.846153 &amp;     0.788347 
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
      <p>Now find the approximations and errors when <m>n=10</m>, <m>n=20</m> and
  <m>n=40</m>.  Record your observations below.</p>
      <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      <m>\Delta x</m> &amp; Approximation &amp; Error 
      \hline
      0.1 &amp;  &amp;   
      0.05 &amp; &amp;  
      0.025 &amp; &amp; 
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
      <p>\item[4.]  Now summarize your results by building a table of the
  errors in the three methods.</p>
      <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
      \hline
      <m>\Delta x</m> &amp; Error in right endpoints &amp; Error in Midpoint Rule &amp; 
      Error in Trapezoidal rule 
      \hline
      0.1 &amp; &amp; &amp; 
      0.05 &amp; &amp; &amp; 
      0.025 &amp; &amp; &amp; 
      \hline
    \end{tabular}</p>
      <p>Describe anything that you notice.  In particular, consider what
  happens when you half <m>\Delta x</m>.  By approximately what factor does
  the error decrease when you use right endpoints?  when you use midpoints?
  when you use trapezoids?   Which method or methods are better for
  approximating definite integrals?  Explain your findings by
  considering how the approximations are constructed geometrically.</p>
      <p>\end{itemize}</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
